\section*{Liouville Brownian motion as the walk limit on FK–Ising maps}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. The stationary vertex walk on the same FK--Ising maps is claimed to converge in actual time to Brownian motion of the $\sqrt3$-Liouville sphere, with edge-count acceleration.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
The spectral companion supplies unit conductivity, local energy recovery, and the coupled surface. Dirichlet-form and inverse-operator estimates give tight path laws and identify the limiting Markov process, retaining conditional independence for any fixed number of walks on that surface.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The stationary vertex walk on the same FK--Ising maps is claimed to converge in actual time to Brownian motion of the $\sqrt3$-Liouville sphere, with edge-count acceleration. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 211, the selected Lean theorem is the $q>4$ Brownian tree metric-measure limit, with degree measure and the $n^{-1/2}$ distance scale. The subcritical, critical-Liouville, walk, and spectral companions are not included. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

